#version 450

// Writes a scattered set of fixed-width records into one of the particle
// system's device-resident tables.
//
// **Three callers, one shader, because they are the same problem.** Every frame
// the host has a handful of rows to place in a table it does not otherwise
// touch: the tick's newborn particles into the state pool, and - only when a
// script or a moving emitter changed something - a block's parameters or its
// curves into the tables the step reads. All three are "here is an index and
// here are N words", so all three are this, with the width in a uniform and the
// destination decided by what is bound.
//
// ## Why the block tables are scattered rather than restaged
//
// A block record is three hundred and eighty-four bytes and the roadmap's scene
// has a hundred thousand emitters. Staging all of them every frame is thirty-
// nine megabytes of writes into a mapped transfer buffer - *more* than the
// sixteen the whole particle pool cost before it moved to the device, which
// would make the move a loss at exactly the scale it exists for.
//
// Almost none of it changes from frame to frame. `EmitterBlock::Revision` says
// when it has, the renderer keeps its own copy of what it last sent, and what
// crosses is the difference: nothing at all for a static scene, and one record
// per emitter that a script wrote or that moved. What is left per frame is the
// two words a draw needs - which block and where its run lands - and those do
// have to be sent every frame, because the sort that decides them is per frame.
//
// ## Why the spawn is here too
//
// A birth goes to whichever slot its block's ring is pointing at, and those are
// as scattered as the emitters are. It was this shader before the block tables
// existed and the generalisation cost it a uniform.
//
// **The host still decides every birth**, and that is not laziness: working out
// where a particle starts reads the emitter's shape, its half-extent, its
// parent's motion and a draw seeded from the emitter's entity. None of that is
// going to the device, and none of it needs to - a steady scene of five thousand
// emitters births about seventeen hundred particles a tick against the half
// million it *ages*.

layout(local_size_x = 64, local_size_y = 1, local_size_z = 1) in;

// Each record is its destination index and then `Words` payload words.
layout(set = 0, binding = 0) readonly buffer Records { uint words[]; } records;

layout(set = 1, binding = 0) buffer Table { uint words[]; } table;

layout(set = 2, binding = 0) uniform Scatter {
	// x: how many records there are. y: how many words each one carries.
	// z: how many rows the destination table holds. w: unused.
	uvec4 Count;
} scatter_;

void main() {
	uint at = gl_GlobalInvocationID.x;
	if (at >= scatter_.Count.x) {
		return;
	}

	uint words = scatter_.Count.y;
	uint base = at * (words + 1u);
	uint row = records.words[base];
	if (row >= scatter_.Count.z) {
		// The host sized the table and the host chose the row, so this cannot
		// happen - but a compute shader that walks off a storage buffer corrupts
		// whatever is next in memory rather than faulting, and the guard is one
		// compare against a value already in a register.
		return;
	}

	uint destination = row * words;
	for (uint word = 0u; word < words; word++) {
		table.words[destination + word] = records.words[base + 1u + word];
	}
}
